From: pixel2 ubuntu port
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: venc: HFI 3xx CFR rate control must ignore timestamps

On msm8998 the encoder ignored the target bitrate: a 1280x720 test
pattern came out at ~16 Mbps whether V4L2_CID_MPEG_VIDEO_BITRATE was
1 or 6 Mbps, VBR or CBR, while fixed QPs (rate control off) and a
raised minimum QP did change the size (pixel2 test 68). Rate control
was holding QP at its floor.

The 3xx firmware's rate control derives the per-frame budget from the
input timestamp deltas unless HFI_PROPERTY_PARAM_VENC_DISABLE_RC_TIMESTAMP
is set (downstream exposes this as RATE_CONTROL_TIMESTAMP_MODE).
GStreamer's v4l2 encoders put the frame number in tv_sec, so frames
look 1 s apart and every frame gets a full second's worth of bits.

In the CFR modes the frame rate is the one set with S_PARM; tell the
firmware to use it instead of the timestamps.

---
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/venc.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/venc.c
@@ -840,6 +840,24 @@
 	ret = hfi_session_set_property(inst, ptype, &rate_control);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * HFI 3xx rate control budgets each frame from the input timestamp
+	 * deltas unless told not to. V4L2 timestamps are opaque cookies
+	 * (GStreamer puts the frame number in tv_sec, 1 s apart), so the
+	 * target bitrate was ignored. The CFR modes take the rate from
+	 * S_PARM; let the firmware use that.
+	 */
+	if (IS_V3(inst->core) &&
+	    (rate_control == HFI_RATE_CONTROL_VBR_CFR ||
+	     rate_control == HFI_RATE_CONTROL_CBR_CFR)) {
+		struct hfi_enable en = { .enable = 1 };
+
+		ptype = HFI_PROPERTY_PARAM_VENC_DISABLE_RC_TIMESTAMP;
+		ret = hfi_session_set_property(inst, ptype, &en);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+	}
 
 	if (rate_control == HFI_RATE_CONTROL_CQ && ctr->const_quality) {
 		struct hfi_heic_frame_quality quality = {};
